(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Formulate the thermodynamic laws governing energy flow in ecological systems, and explain why ecosystems are non-equilibrium dissipative structures.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Thermodynamics of Ecosystem Energetics and Dissipative Structure Theory:) Tj
T*
(   1. First Law of Thermodynamics \(Conservation of Energy\):) Tj
T*
(   - Principle: Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form into) Tj
T*
(   another.) Tj
T*
(   - Ecological Manifestation: Total incident solar radiant energy hitting an ecosystem equals the) Tj
T*
(   sum of energy converted into chemical bond energy via photosynthesis \(Gross Primary) Tj
T*
(   Production\), reflected as shortwave albedo, absorbed as sensible heat warming the) Tj
T*
(   soil/atmosphere, and driving latent heat of water evapotranspiration:) Tj
T*
(   E_solar = E_biomass + E_albedo + E_sensible + E_latent.) Tj
T*
(   2. Second Law of Thermodynamics \(Entropy Escalation\):) Tj
T*
(   - Principle: Any spontaneous process results in an increase in the total entropy of the) Tj
T*
(   universe; no thermodynamic energy transfer is 100% efficient.) Tj
T*
(   - Ecological Manifestation: In every metabolic conversion \(photosynthesis, herbivory, cellular) Tj
T*
(   respiration\), a large fraction of high-grade free energy is degraded into low-temperature,) Tj
T*
(   disordered thermal energy \(heat\). Heat cannot be re-utilized by autotrophs to drive chemical) Tj
T*
(   synthesis; hence, energy does NOT cycle through ecosystems-it flows unidirectionally from a) Tj
T*
(   high-quality solar source to an entropic outer-space heat sink.) Tj
T*
(   3. Prigogine's Dissipative Structure Theory:) Tj
T*
(   Living ecosystems maintain highly ordered, complex structural and organizational states \(low) Tj
T*
(   internal entropy\) far from thermodynamic equilibrium. They achieve this open-system steady) Tj
T*
(   state by continuously importing low-entropy solar energy and exporting high-entropy metabolic) Tj
T*
(   heat to their surrounding environment.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define Gross Primary Productivity \(GPP\), Net Primary Productivity \(NPP\), and Autotrophic Respiration \(R\), and detail the Light-and-Dark Bottle technique.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Primary Productivity Dynamics and the Light-and-Dark Bottle Measurement Protocol:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mathematical Formulations:) Tj
T*
(   - Gross Primary Productivity \(GPP\): The total rate of organic matter synthesis \(or chemical) Tj
T*
(   energy capture\) by autotrophic organisms through photosynthesis per unit area per unit time:) Tj
T*
(   Units: g C/m2/yr or kcal/m2/yr.) Tj
T*
(   - Autotrophic Respiration \(R_a\): The rate of metabolic energy expenditure by primary producers) Tj
T*
(   to support cellular maintenance, active nutrient transport, and protein synthesis:) Tj
T*
(   Units: g C/m2/yr.) Tj
T*
(   - Net Primary Productivity \(NPP\): The net rate of biomass accumulation available for) Tj
T*
(   consumption by heterotrophic herbivores and decomposers:) Tj
T*
(   NPP = GPP - R_a) Tj
T*
(   2. The Gaarder and Gran Light-and-Dark Bottle Aquatic Technique:) Tj
T*
(   - Methodology:) Tj
T*
(   Three identical borosilicate glass bottles are filled with representative pond or lake water) Tj
T*
(   containing phytoplankton:) Tj
T*
(   * Initial Bottle \(IB\): Fixed immediately with Winkler chemical reagents to determine baseline) Tj
T*
(   Dissolved Oxygen \(DO_initial\).) Tj
T*
(   * Light Bottle \(LB\): Transparent glass allowing sunlight penetration; incubated in situ at) Tj
T*
(   depth for time Delta t \(typically 24 hours\). Both photosynthesis and cellular respiration occur) Tj
T*
(   simultaneously.) Tj
T*
ET

