45 780 Td
(   2. Connell and Slatyer Models of Succession \(1977\):) Tj
T*
(   Joseph Connell and Ralph Slatyer proposed three alternative pathways governing species) Tj
T*
(   turnover:) Tj
T*
(   - Facilitation Model:) Tj
T*
(   * Mechanism: Pioneer colonizers modify the abiotic environment \(ameliorating extreme) Tj
T*
(   temperatures, fixing atmospheric nitrogen, weathering rock, accumulating organic humus\), making) Tj
T*
(   conditions more favorable for subsequent seral species.) Tj
T*
(   * Outcome: Early pioneers prepare the ground for later-stage species, which eventually) Tj
T*
(   outcompete and displace the pioneers. Typical in primary succession on bare rock, sand dunes,) Tj
T*
(   or glacial moraines.) Tj
T*
(   - Tolerance Model:) Tj
T*
(   * Mechanism: Modifications of the physical environment by early colonizers have neither) Tj
T*
(   positive nor negative effects on the recruitment of subsequent seral species.) Tj
T*
(   * Outcome: Later seral species establish because they are capable of tolerating lower resource) Tj
T*
(   thresholds \(e.g., higher shade tolerance, lower available nitrogen or moisture\) than early) Tj
T*
(   species.) Tj
T*
(   - Inhibition Model:) Tj
T*
(   * Mechanism: The earliest colonizers monopolize spatial territory, light, and nutrients,) Tj
T*
(   actively inhibiting the germination, establishment, and growth of all subsequent invaders) Tj
T*
(   through dense root mats, canopy shading, or allelopathic chemical excretion.) Tj
T*
(   * Outcome: Later species establish ONLY when existing pioneer individuals die due to) Tj
T*
(   senescence, physical disturbance, frost, or pest outbreaks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 7:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the sequential seral stages of Hydrarch \(Hydrosere\) Ecological Succession, from an oligotrophic lake to a mature climax forest.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Sequential Seral Stages of Hydrarch Ecological Succession \(Hydrosere\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Initiation in Open Water:) Tj
T*
(   A freshly formed, deep, nutrient-poor \(oligotrophic\) water body undergoes progressive) Tj
T*
(   biological colonization and allogenic/autogenic sediment filling:) Tj
T*
(   2. Chronological Seral Stages:) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 1: Phytoplankton Stage: Pioneer unicellular planktonic algae \(diatoms, cyanobacteria,) Tj
T*
(   chlorophytes\) multiply in the limnetic zone. As plankton die, their detritus accumulates with) Tj
T*
(   washed-in silt on the barren lake floor, forming a fine organic-mineral substrate.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 2: Submerged Vegetation Stage: When lake depth silts up to 2-3 meters, sufficient) Tj
T*
(   sunlight penetrates the benthic floor, allowing rooted submerged macrophytes \(Hydrilla,) Tj
T*
(   Potamogeton, Vallisneria\) to establish. Their dense underwater foliage traps waterborne) Tj
T*
(   suspended sediments, accelerating bottom accretion.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 3: Floating Macrophyte Stage: At water depths of 1-1.5 meters, rooted broad-leaved) Tj
T*
(   floating plants \(Nymphaea, Nelumbo\) and free-floating species \(Eichhornia, Lemna\) dominate.) Tj
T*
(   Broad floating leaves shade out submerged species, while massive decaying leaf biomass rapidly) Tj
T*
(   shallows the pond.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 4: Reed-Swamp Stage \(Amphibious Phase\): Water depth reaches 0.3-0.8 meters. Emergent) Tj
T*
(   amphibious macrophytes with creeping rhizomes \(Typha latifolia, Phragmites australis\) form) Tj
T*
(   dense reed beds. High transpiration rates transpire water rapidly into the atmosphere, while) Tj
T*
(   trapped sediment converts the basin into a marsh.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 5: Marsh-Meadow Stage: Water recedes to saturated mud. Sedge and grass species \(Carex,) Tj
T*
(   Juncus, Cyperus\) invade, forming a dense terrestrial sod. Soil aeration improves, and organic) Tj
T*
(   peat undergoes aerobic humification.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 6: Woodland / Shrub Stage: Soil dries sufficiently for woody shrubs \(Salix, Alnus\) to) Tj
T*
(   take root. Shrubs cast deep shade, eliminating marsh grasses, while deep root networks draw) Tj
T*
(   down the water table.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
