(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the biogeochemical Carbon Cycle, detailing oceanic carbonate chemistry, the biological pump, and the oceanic carbonate buffer system.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Global Biogeochemical Carbon Cycle and Oceanic Carbonate Chemistry:) Tj
T*
(   1. Carbon Reservoirs and Atmospheric Fluxes:) Tj
T*
(   - Terrestrial Biosphere: Contains ~600 Gt C in living biomass and ~1,500 Gt C in soil humus.) Tj
T*
(   - Atmosphere: Contains ~870 Gt C \(increasing at ~4-5 Gt C/yr due to fossil fuel combustion and) Tj
T*
(   land-use change\).) Tj
T*
(   - Lithosphere: Largest global reservoir \(> 60,000,000 Gt C in sedimentary carbonate rocks like) Tj
T*
(   limestone and chalk, and ~4,000 Gt C in fossil fuel deposits\).) Tj
T*
(   - Hydrosphere \(Oceans\): Contains ~38,000 Gt C, predominantly as dissolved inorganic carbon) Tj
T*
(   \(DIC\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Oceanic Carbonate Buffer Equilibrium:) Tj
T*
(   Atmospheric CO2 gas dissolves in surface seawater according to Henry's Law:) Tj
T*
(   CO2 \(g\) <-> CO2 \(aq\)) Tj
T*
(   Dissolved CO2 hydrates to form carbonic acid, which rapidly dissociates in a two-step acid-base) Tj
T*
(   equilibrium:) Tj
T*
(   CO2 \(aq\) + H2O <-> H2CO3 <-> H+ + HCO3- \(Bicarbonate\)) Tj
T*
(   HCO3- <-> H+ + CO3^2- \(Carbonate\)) Tj
T*
(   In typical modern surface seawater \(pH ~ 8.1\), DIC speciation is: ~89% HCO3-, ~10% CO3^2-, and) Tj
T*
(   only ~1% aqueous CO2/H2CO3.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Ocean Biological and Carbonate Pumps:) Tj
T*
(   - Organic Biological Carbon Pump: Photosynthetic phytoplankton in the euphotic zone convert) Tj
T*
(   dissolved CO2 into organic carbon. A fraction of dead biomass aggregates as 'marine snow' and) Tj
T*
(   sinks into the bathypelagic and benthic abyss, sequestering carbon for centuries.) Tj
T*
(   - Carbonate Counter-Pump: Calcifying organisms \(coccolithophores, foraminifera, pteropods,) Tj
T*
(   corals\) secrete calcium carbonate shells:) Tj
T*
(   Ca2+ + 2 HCO3- <-> CaCO3 \(s\) + CO2 + H2O) Tj
T*
(   Precipitation of CaCO3 paradoxically releases CO2 back into solution! When shells sink below) Tj
T*
(   the Calcite Compensation Depth \(CCD\), high hydrostatic pressure and low temperature re-dissolve) Tj
T*
(   calcium carbonate.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
