(   - K: Hydraulic conductivity of the porous geological medium \(m/s or m/day\), reflecting the ease) Tj
T*
(   with which water moves through pore spaces.) Tj
T*
(   - A: Total cross-sectional area perpendicular to the flow path \(m2\).) Tj
T*
(   - dh/dl: Hydraulic gradient \(dimensionless\), where dh is the change in hydraulic head) Tj
T*
(   \(piezometric level\) over distance dl. The negative sign signifies that groundwater flows in the) Tj
T*
(   direction of decreasing hydraulic potential.) Tj
T*
(   2. Intrinsic Permeability vs Hydraulic Conductivity:) Tj
T*
(   K is related to the intrinsic permeability k of the rock matrix by:) Tj
T*
(   K = \(k * rho * g\) / mu) Tj
T*
(   Where rho is fluid density, g is gravitational acceleration, and mu is dynamic viscosity.) Tj
T*
(   Coarse gravels and karstic limestones have high K \(10^2 to 10^4 m/day\), while clays and shales) Tj
T*
(   have very low K \(10^-7 to 10^-4 m/day, acting as aquitards\).) Tj
T*
(   3. The Pumping Cone of Depression:) Tj
T*
(   - When an abstraction well pumps water at volumetric rate Q, hydraulic head in the well casing) Tj
T*
(   drops below the surrounding water table.) Tj
T*
(   - This creates a radial convergent hydraulic gradient toward the wellbore, warping the) Tj
T*
(   initially flat water table into a three-dimensional curved funnel called the Cone of) Tj
T*
(   Depression.) Tj
T*
(   - The radius of influence \(R\) expands outwards until the rate of radial recharge across the) Tj
T*
(   cone perimeter balances the extraction rate Q.) Tj
T*
(   - Over-pumping \(extraction exceeding safe yield\) causes progressive regional water table) Tj
T*
(   decline, dewatering of neighboring shallow wells, land subsidence, and stream depletion.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
